			<item>
				<title>Variogram Models</title>
				<link>https://spatlyu.github.io/posts/variogram_models/</link>
				<pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>https://spatlyu.github.io/posts/variogram_models/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;variogram-models&#34;&gt;Variogram models&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The variogram describes how spatial dissimilarity changes with the separation distance between locations. The theoretical semivariogram is defined as&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;$$&#xA;\gamma(h) = \frac{1}{2}&#xA;\operatorname{Var}\left[Z(\mathbf{s})-Z(\mathbf{s}+\mathbf{h})\right],&#xA;$$&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;where $Z(\mathbf{s})$ is a spatial random field, $\mathbf{h}$ is a spatial lag vector, and $h=|\mathbf{h}|$ is the lag distance. Under second-order stationarity, the semivariogram is related to the covariance function by&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;$$&#xA;\gamma(h)=C(0)-C(h).&#xA;$$&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A common variogram model with a nugget effect is written as&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
